Theming/MaterialSurface.cs
Copyright 2024 The Android Open Source Project
Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. See licenses/Apache-2.0.txt.
Adapted to C#/Avalonia from AndroidX Material 3 at commit
77b2199d6f056c2fe829ce25523c34a905e769c1:
- Surface.kt:100-130 (absolute tonal elevation = inherited absolute + local tonalElevation) and
  Surface.kt:563-590 (the content-color Surface overload: LocalAbsoluteTonalElevation is
  provided to the subtree, the tonal overlay is driven by that absolute elevation while
  shadowElevation only drives the graphics-layer shadow);
- ColorScheme.kt:1146-1160 (surfaceColorAtElevation: one logarithmic tint overlay);
- ColorScheme.kt:1546-1590 (applyTonalElevation and LocalTonalElevationEnabled: the tint is
  applied only when the component color is exactly the surface role and tonal elevation is
  enabled).

Kotlin composition locals have no Avalonia equivalent. The inherited absolute tonal elevation is
therefore summed from the native visual (with a logical fallback) parent chain on demand: a
nested surface reads its ancestors' current tonal elevation without a propagation copy, so a
theme, seed or ancestor change is observed on the next refresh and never goes stale. The overlay
is a single tint of the exact surface role; it is not a second translucent layer, so an ancestor
and a descendant cannot double-tint the same pixels.


